.TH "TDDA DETECT" 1 "May 2026" "3.0" "tdda detect manual"
.SH NAME
\fBtdda detect\fR — Detect data that does not obey supplied constraints

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtdda detect\fR [-h] [-?] [-7] [--no-config] [--colour] [--no-colour]
            [-epsilon EPSILON] [-o REPORT_PATH] [-a] [-f]
            [-t {strict,loose}] [--write-all-records]
            [--per-constraint] [--no-per-constraint]
            [--no-original-fields] [--original-fields]
            [--no-output-fields] [--output-fields [OUTPUT_FIELDS ...]]
            [-r [REPORT ...]] [--interleave] [--no-interleave]
            [--index] [--int] [--key [KEY ...]]
            [--verify-required-fields] [--verify-allowed-fields]
            [--no-verify-required-fields] [--no-verify-allowed-fields]
            [--varf] [--no-varf] [--pandas] [--polars]
            [--backend BACKEND]
            \fIINPUT\fP [\fICONSTRAINTS\fP [\fIOUTPUT\fP]]

.SH POSITIONAL ARGUMENTS
\fIINPUT\fP is one of:
  - a CSV file or other flat file (e.g. \fB\&.csv\fR, \fB\&.txt\fR, \fB\&.psv\fR),
    optionally using \fB:\fR format to specify flat-file metadata
    (see the help for \fBtdda serial\fR)
  - a data frame in a Parquet file (\fB\&.parquet\fR)
    e.g. from pandas, polars, R
  - a table from PostgreSQL databases (e.g. \fBpostgres:tablename\fR)
  - a table from MySQL databases (e.g. \fBmysql:tablename\fR)
  - a table from SQLite databases (e.g. \fBsqlite:tablename\fR)
  - Standard input (stdin): Use \fB-\fR to read from stdin

.PP
\fICONSTRAINTS\fP, if provided, is a JSON \fB\&.tdda\fR file containing
constraints.

.PP
If no constraints file is provided, a file with the same path as
the input file, with a \fB\&.tdda\fR extension will be tried.

.PP
\fIOUTPUT\fP specifies the destination for detected records.

.PP
This is usually a file if the input was a file (e.g. a \fB\&.csv\fR
file or a \fBparquet\fR file), but does not have to be the same type.
If the input is a database table, the output is always a database
table in the same database.

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBtdda detect\fR command finds and reports data that fails to satisfy
the constraints in the \fICONSTRAINTS\fP file specified. It also performs all
the same functions as \fBtdda verify\fR\&.

.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fR, \fB--help\fR              Show this help message and exit
.br
\fB-?\fR, \fB--?\fR                 Same as \fB-h\fR or \fB--help\fR
.br
\fB-7\fR, \fB--ascii\fR             Report without using special characters
.br
\fB-N\fR, \fB--no-config\fR         Skip loading \fB~/.tdda.toml\fR

.PP
\fB--colour\fR                Use colour in terminal output
.br
\fB--no-colour\fR             Do not use colour in terminal output

.PP
\fB--epsilon\fR \fIEPSILON\fP       Epsilon fuzziness (tolerance for comparisons)

.PP
\fB-a\fR, \fB--all\fR               Report all fields, even if there are no
.br
                        failures
.br
\fB-f\fR, \fB--fields\fR            Report only fields with failures

.PP
\fB-r\fR, \fB--report\fR [\fIREPORT\fP ...]
.br
                        Report formats to write, space-separated.
.br
                        Formats: \fBhtml\fR, \fBmd\fR (\fBmarkdown\fR), \fBtxt\fR (\fBtext\fR),
.br
                        \fBjson\fR, \fByaml\fR, \fBtoml\fR\&.
.br
                        The stem of the output file is taken from
.br
                        \fIREPORT_PATH\fP if \fB-o\fR is given, otherwise from
.br
                        \fIOUTPUT\fP\&.
.br
\fB-t\fR, \fB--type_checking\fR {\fIstrict\fP,\fIloose\fP}
.br
                        "loose" means consider all numeric types
.br
                        equivalent
.br
\fB-o\fR, \fB--report-path\fR \fIREPORT_PATH\fP
.br
                        Stem path for report files (extension is
                        replaced by the format).

.PP
\fB--write-all-records\fR   Include passing records
.br
\fB--per-constraint\fR      Write one flag column per failing constraint in
.br
                        addition to n_failures. Set by default.
.br
\fB--no-per-constraint\fR   Do not write out any per-constraint flag columns
.br
\fB--no-original-fields\fR  Do not write out original fields columns
.br
\fB--original-fields\fR     Write out original fields columns (default)
.br
\fB--no-output-fields\fR    Do not write out any original fields in the output. By
.br
                        default, all original columns will be included.
.br
\fB--output-fields\fR [\fIOUTPUT_FIELDS\fP ...]
.br
                        Specify original columns to write out.
.br
\fB--interleave\fR          Interleave ok columns with original fields.
.br
\fB--no-interleave\fR       Do not interleave ok columns with original fields.
.br
\fB--index\fR               Include a row-number index in the output file when
.br
                        detecting. Rows are usually numbered from 1,
.br
                        unless the input file already has an index.
.br
\fB--int\fR                 Write out boolean fields as integers, with 1 for true
.br
                        and 0 for false.
.br
\fB--key [KEY ...]\fR       Key or key fields to use when reporting failures

.PP
\fB--verify-required-fields\fR, \fB--vrf\fR
.br
                        Force verify of required fields
.br
\fB--verify-allowed-fields\fR, \fB--vaf\fR
.br
                        Force verify of allowed fields
.br
\fB--no-verify-required-fields\fR, \fB--no-vrf\fR
.br
                        Force no verication of required fields
.br
\fB--no-verify-allowed-fields\fR, \fB--no-vaf\fR
.br
                        Force no verification of allowed fields
.br
\fB--varf\fR, \fB--vraf\fR          Force verification of allowed and required
.br
                        fields
.br
\fB--no-varf\fR, \fB--no-vraf\fR    Force no verification of allowed and required
.br
                        fields

.PP
\fB--pandas\fR, \fB--pd\fR          Use Pandas as DataFrame engine.
.br
\fB--polars\fR, \fB--pl\fR          Use Polars as DataFrame engine.
.br
\fB--backend\fR, \fB-B\fR \fIBACKEND\fP   Backend choice for Pandas
.br
                        (when dataframe engine is Pandas)
.br
                          \fBn\fR for numpy_nullable *
.br
                          \fBa\fR for pyarrow
.br
                          \fBo\fR for original.

.SH EXAMPLES
The example data can be obtained by running \fBtdda examples\fR, which will
create various directories, including \fBconstraints_examples\fR, containing
source data for these examples.

.PP
1) \fBtdda detect elements.parquet elements.tdda elements-failures.parquet\fR

.PP
This command reads data from \fBelements.parquet\fR, checks it against the
constraints in \fBelements.tdda\fR, and writes records with one or more
constraint failures to \fBelements-failures.parquet\fR\&.

.PP
2) \fBtdda detect elements.parquet elements.tdda elements-failures.parquet -r html -o elements\fR

.PP
As above, and also writes an HTML report to \fBelements.html\fR\&.

.PP
3) \fBtdda detect elements.parquet elements.tdda elements-failures.parquet -r md json txt -o elements\fR

.PP
As above, and also writes reports to \fBelements.md\fR, \fBelements.json\fR,
and \fBelements.txt\fR\&.

.SH SEE ALSO
\fBtdda-verify(1)\fR,
\fBtdda-discover(1)\fR,
\fBtdda-serial(1)\fR
